1
00:00:01,398 --> 00:00:02,388
Hi, everyone.

2
00:00:02,398 --> 00:00:04,157
My name is Ruben Marcos.

3
00:00:04,788 --> 00:00:06,438
I'm a full stack AI engineer.

4
00:00:06,458 --> 00:00:11,018
I have been shipping software for about
fourteen years, and for the last couple

5
00:00:11,028 --> 00:00:15,338
of years, most of the code I ship has
been written inside an AI coding loop.

6
00:00:16,398 --> 00:00:21,908
So this talk is about one thing, the
thing that decides whether the code

7
00:00:21,908 --> 00:00:24,477
is real or whether it is fiction.

8
00:00:26,038 --> 00:00:27,598
And spoiler, it's not the model

9
00:00:28,598 --> 00:00:38,068
The incident on August 7 I was Facing
an issue that I wanted to rather forget

10
00:00:39,068 --> 00:00:42,077
I run a browser FPS called CS Brazil.

11
00:00:43,647 --> 00:00:46,608
A couple thousand players, 27 countries.

12
00:00:47,698 --> 00:00:49,138
The client is open source

13
00:00:50,138 --> 00:00:52,297
That night, our refactor lands.

14
00:00:52,687 --> 00:00:58,418
Type check passes, the build passes, our
deploy gate passes, everything is green,

15
00:00:59,718 --> 00:01:04,947
and then the play button dies Every
player who opens the game sees a red

16
00:01:04,967 --> 00:01:07,257
banner and a button that does nothing.

17
00:01:08,747 --> 00:01:14,267
The cause was a temporal dead zone, a
const being read before it was initialized

18
00:01:14,657 --> 00:01:16,867
because the bundle order had changed.

19
00:01:17,957 --> 00:01:20,917
That kind of error doesn't
exist at compile time.

20
00:01:20,977 --> 00:01:24,327
It only exists at runtime,
on that path, in that order.

21
00:01:26,327 --> 00:01:30,917
And the part that still bothers me,
the game painted that red banner,

22
00:01:31,327 --> 00:01:36,557
and it reported nowhere, no error
tracker, no log line, no metric.

23
00:01:36,637 --> 00:01:38,087
We found out from players.

24
00:01:39,757 --> 00:01:45,897
Green in CI, broken in prod, and the
pipeline was structurally unable to know

25
00:01:46,897 --> 00:01:47,797
What is fiction?

26
00:01:48,797 --> 00:01:51,537
That incident is one example
of a bigger category.

27
00:01:51,997 --> 00:01:53,097
I call it fiction.

28
00:01:54,107 --> 00:01:58,587
Output that is plausible,
well-formed, confident, and wrong.

29
00:01:59,937 --> 00:02:02,587
And there's a reason it looks like that.

30
00:02:03,207 --> 00:02:08,347
A language model is optimized to
produce the most likely n-next

31
00:02:08,347 --> 00:02:10,177
token, not the most true one.

32
00:02:12,147 --> 00:02:14,667
Plausibility is the objective.

33
00:02:15,307 --> 00:02:19,417
Truth is a side effect most
of the time, so the output

34
00:02:19,417 --> 00:02:21,087
is confident by construction.

35
00:02:22,347 --> 00:02:26,477
The model has no idea when it's
guessing because guessing and knowing

36
00:02:26,567 --> 00:02:28,537
f-feel identical from the inside.

37
00:02:30,097 --> 00:02:33,757
We've all seen the shapes, the
import that doesn't exist, but looks

38
00:02:33,817 --> 00:02:35,527
exactly like the one that should.

39
00:02:36,498 --> 00:02:40,347
The fix for a flaky test
that deletes the test.

40
00:02:41,827 --> 00:02:45,368
The migration that work on
an empty temble-- table.

41
00:02:46,437 --> 00:02:49,207
The test that pass because
they assert the mock.

42
00:02:51,137 --> 00:02:54,538
The number and the final answer
that no tool ever computed.

43
00:02:55,757 --> 00:02:58,057
The model is not lying, it's completing.

44
00:02:58,317 --> 00:02:59,428
Completing its job.

45
00:02:59,857 --> 00:03:00,497
It's the job.

46
00:03:01,967 --> 00:03:06,267
And the more fluent the model
gets, the better the fiction reads

47
00:03:07,267 --> 00:03:10,387
So look what the pipeline actually knew.

48
00:03:10,907 --> 00:03:12,067
Type check, yes.

49
00:03:12,067 --> 00:03:12,937
Build, yes.

50
00:03:12,967 --> 00:03:13,707
Gate, yes.

51
00:03:13,727 --> 00:03:14,617
Review, yes.

52
00:03:15,237 --> 00:03:19,167
And not one of them could tell
works from look like it works.

53
00:03:20,757 --> 00:03:22,987
Here's the general principle behind that.

54
00:03:23,167 --> 00:03:26,617
Almost every check in a
pipeline is a check on form.

55
00:03:27,637 --> 00:03:28,517
Does it parse?

56
00:03:28,527 --> 00:03:29,227
Does it type?

57
00:03:29,257 --> 00:03:31,447
Does it-- the diff look reasonable?

58
00:03:32,687 --> 00:03:34,847
Human review is a check on form too.

59
00:03:34,947 --> 00:03:38,477
A tired reviewer at six PM
reads shape, not behavior.

60
00:03:39,887 --> 00:03:44,137
That was fine when the author was a person
who also understood what the code did.

61
00:03:45,607 --> 00:03:49,727
Once the author is a model, form is
exactly the thing that's always perfect.

62
00:03:50,607 --> 00:03:54,807
So every check on form stop
discriminating at all at that same time.

63
00:03:55,767 --> 00:03:58,077
What's left is checks on behavior

64
00:03:59,077 --> 00:04:00,217
And that's the question.

65
00:04:01,427 --> 00:04:05,747
For the rest of this talk, what is in your
system actually checking the behavior?

66
00:04:06,747 --> 00:04:11,657
The model is not the reliability layer
The first instinct is to fix the model.

67
00:04:11,667 --> 00:04:15,937
Bigger model, better prompt, more
context, a reasoning model-- mode.

68
00:04:16,887 --> 00:04:18,737
And all of that moves the error rate.

69
00:04:19,307 --> 00:04:21,407
None of it changes the shape of failure.

70
00:04:22,107 --> 00:04:25,697
This is an old lesson in
engineering, not a new one.

71
00:04:26,057 --> 00:04:29,917
Reliability never comes from the
average quality of a component.

72
00:04:30,517 --> 00:04:32,537
It comes from the structure around it.

73
00:04:33,427 --> 00:04:37,117
We didn't make cars safe by
asking for better drivers.

74
00:04:37,157 --> 00:04:39,987
We added brakes, belts, and airbags.

75
00:04:40,647 --> 00:04:45,407
Databases aren't consistent
because developers are careful.

76
00:04:45,567 --> 00:04:47,867
They have constraints and transactions.

77
00:04:48,647 --> 00:04:51,647
And every team already
knows this from experience.

78
00:04:52,237 --> 00:04:54,867
"Let's all be more careful" never works.

79
00:04:54,867 --> 00:04:58,757
Linters, typers, and CI did.

80
00:04:58,757 --> 00:05:03,337
Two percent fiction at two hundred
mirrors a week is four incidents a week.

81
00:05:03,337 --> 00:05:07,447
So the question is not: how do
I make the model trustworthy?

82
00:05:07,617 --> 00:05:10,937
It's: what in my system
decides what to trust?

83
00:05:12,587 --> 00:05:13,727
The harder it is

84
00:05:14,727 --> 00:05:16,187
And that thing is the harness.

85
00:05:16,217 --> 00:05:20,957
Everything around the model,
the types is, it has to speak.

86
00:05:22,157 --> 00:05:27,257
Validation at every boundary
Guardrails that say no by default.

87
00:05:28,347 --> 00:05:31,587
An eval gate that blocks regressions.

88
00:05:33,077 --> 00:05:36,807
And a trace that records
what actually happened.

89
00:05:37,217 --> 00:05:38,077
It's not a prompt.

90
00:05:38,237 --> 00:05:43,177
It's not a system message saying, "Please
be careful and don't make things up."

91
00:05:44,277 --> 00:05:48,327
It's code, and it runs the same way
whether your model is careful or not.

92
00:05:49,667 --> 00:05:53,037
Everything the model produces
passes through it before

93
00:05:53,037 --> 00:05:54,537
it touches anything real.

94
00:05:55,247 --> 00:05:59,247
So the harness decides what to
trust, not the model, not the

95
00:05:59,247 --> 00:06:01,307
reviewer at two in the morning.

96
00:06:01,807 --> 00:06:07,187
Now, instead of letting you through
five layers, I'm going to break

97
00:06:07,247 --> 00:06:11,547
a small harness three times and
show you what catches it each time

98
00:06:12,547 --> 00:06:13,557
Here's the setup.

99
00:06:14,947 --> 00:06:20,407
A demo, a small typed harness in
TypeScript built for this talk.

100
00:06:21,177 --> 00:06:24,837
It runs on a deterministic step
provider, so there is no API key,

101
00:06:24,877 --> 00:06:28,107
and what you are about to see is
the harness, not the model look.

102
00:06:29,287 --> 00:06:30,107
Three bits.

103
00:06:30,107 --> 00:06:34,117
The next slides are the real output of
three NPM scripts from the repo, which

104
00:06:34,117 --> 00:06:39,107
is linked at the end, so you can run
them yourself First be the model ships

105
00:06:39,107 --> 00:06:41,647
fiction And we can run the fiction.

106
00:06:42,007 --> 00:06:46,937
First, the model asks for a tool that
doesn't exist, deploy to prod, and you

107
00:06:46,937 --> 00:06:49,107
get a typed failure, invalid output.

108
00:06:49,317 --> 00:06:50,617
Zero tools executed.

109
00:06:50,717 --> 00:06:51,927
The loop didn't guess.

110
00:06:51,997 --> 00:06:52,667
It stopped.

111
00:06:53,477 --> 00:06:58,027
Second, the model calls their
calculator with garbage arguments.

112
00:06:58,847 --> 00:07:01,767
Twelve times they drop stable users

113
00:07:02,767 --> 00:07:04,247
The boundary rejects them.

114
00:07:04,357 --> 00:07:05,307
Invalid args.

115
00:07:05,357 --> 00:07:08,437
And then the model says the
answer is ninety-six anyway.

116
00:07:08,467 --> 00:07:12,607
So the prose says ninety-six, and
the trace says the calculator n- the

117
00:07:12,607 --> 00:07:13,987
calculator never returned a value.

118
00:07:14,017 --> 00:07:15,177
Which one do you ship?

119
00:07:16,827 --> 00:07:17,837
Third, the same run.

120
00:07:17,837 --> 00:07:19,367
Score the way CI scored it.

121
00:07:19,797 --> 00:07:21,357
Final answer includes ninety-six.

122
00:07:21,357 --> 00:07:21,757
Check.

123
00:07:21,957 --> 00:07:22,827
Calls calculator.

124
00:07:22,827 --> 00:07:23,287
Fail.

125
00:07:23,337 --> 00:07:24,847
Call but never return okay.

126
00:07:24,877 --> 00:07:27,097
The scorer reads the
trace, not the answer.

127
00:07:27,697 --> 00:07:31,607
Fiction that reads well still
fails the gate What got it?

128
00:07:32,607 --> 00:07:35,707
Typed contracts, validated boundary.

129
00:07:37,577 --> 00:07:38,437
So what caught it?

130
00:07:38,787 --> 00:07:42,337
Two things, and both are ideas
older than language models.

131
00:07:42,367 --> 00:07:46,787
The first one is make illegal
states unrepresentable.

132
00:07:47,737 --> 00:07:51,697
Everything the model can say to the
harness is a discriminated union.

133
00:07:51,817 --> 00:07:54,637
A step is a tool call or a final answer.

134
00:07:54,717 --> 00:07:58,797
A result is OK with a value
or not OK with a typed error.

135
00:07:59,457 --> 00:08:03,027
You cannot read the value
without proving OK is true.

136
00:08:03,767 --> 00:08:05,517
The compiler forces the error path.

137
00:08:06,907 --> 00:08:09,477
Everything has the other version of this.

138
00:08:10,627 --> 00:08:14,837
The status field that's a string
and now has fourteen spellings.

139
00:08:15,977 --> 00:08:18,107
The error handling that does.

140
00:08:18,397 --> 00:08:20,087
If message includes timeout…

141
00:08:22,087 --> 00:08:24,977
Those are strings, and nobody
branches on strings reliably.

142
00:08:25,817 --> 00:08:30,037
Here, every failure has a name, and every
name is a branch someone had to write.

143
00:08:31,757 --> 00:08:35,907
The second one is the oldest rule
in web security: Never trust input.

144
00:08:36,777 --> 00:08:39,567
We all parse, parse from
data from the Internet.

145
00:08:39,657 --> 00:08:41,797
The model's arguments are the same thing.

146
00:08:42,917 --> 00:08:45,167
Every tool has a ZOD schema.

147
00:08:45,167 --> 00:08:48,287
Bad arguments become
invalid args, never a crash.

148
00:08:48,377 --> 00:08:49,887
And notice the subtle part.

149
00:08:50,457 --> 00:08:53,757
A validation failure is an
observation, not an exception.

150
00:08:54,487 --> 00:08:58,627
The model sees it, it can correct
itself, and the trace remembers

151
00:08:58,637 --> 00:09:00,017
that the tool never returned.

152
00:09:00,577 --> 00:09:02,127
If it's not parsed, it's not trusted.

153
00:09:03,127 --> 00:09:04,217
B2, flail closed.

154
00:09:04,217 --> 00:09:04,257
NPM run demo gate.

155
00:09:04,257 --> 00:09:04,667
Same goal, twice save a note.

156
00:09:04,787 --> 00:09:07,097
First run, default policy, refused.

157
00:09:07,697 --> 00:09:10,887
There is a policy denying the
trace, and the model has to say

158
00:09:10,887 --> 00:09:12,457
honestly that it couldn't save.

159
00:09:13,097 --> 00:09:16,287
Second run, a human sets
allowsWrite to true.

160
00:09:16,887 --> 00:09:19,727
Now the write runs, and
there is a true result, okay.

161
00:09:20,617 --> 00:09:23,907
The write happened because our
human turned the flag on, not

162
00:09:23,907 --> 00:09:25,697
because the model asked nicely

163
00:09:26,697 --> 00:09:28,537
What coded the fail closed?

164
00:09:30,087 --> 00:09:33,957
What coded wha-- That one is the
policy and ID is default deny.

165
00:09:34,567 --> 00:09:36,457
Firewalls works like this.

166
00:09:36,567 --> 00:09:38,047
IAM works like this.

167
00:09:38,857 --> 00:09:42,087
A good feature flag is off
until someone turns it on.

168
00:09:42,737 --> 00:09:45,897
Every tool declares whether
it mutates the rule.

169
00:09:46,107 --> 00:09:50,807
If it does and policy has not
explicit say yes, the loop refuses,

170
00:09:50,937 --> 00:09:53,247
it refuses and tells the model why.

171
00:09:53,807 --> 00:09:58,037
There's a loop cap, so a confusion
model can't spin forever.

172
00:09:59,707 --> 00:10:04,837
And a per-step timeouts, so a run
provider becomes a type timeout

173
00:10:05,297 --> 00:10:06,827
instead of a stuck process.

174
00:10:07,737 --> 00:10:10,297
Most of us wire things
the other way around.

175
00:10:10,407 --> 00:10:13,087
We build it to work,
and then we add checks.

176
00:10:14,397 --> 00:10:15,997
And you know how that ends.

177
00:10:16,057 --> 00:10:19,907
The deploy script that skips the
health check when the endpoint

178
00:10:19,917 --> 00:10:23,817
is missing, the cron job that
happily runs with a empty config.

179
00:10:25,147 --> 00:10:29,367
Fail opens feels convenient
right up to the incident.

180
00:10:29,467 --> 00:10:35,307
A fail close harness is built to refuse,
and yes, has to be earned with evidence.

181
00:10:36,247 --> 00:10:38,097
And the policy is data, not code.

182
00:10:38,207 --> 00:10:38,957
A human sets it per-- sets each per run.

183
00:10:38,957 --> 00:10:42,057
The eval data set sets it per case.

184
00:10:42,567 --> 00:10:46,407
The loop never gets to decide
wh- that a write is fine.

185
00:10:48,167 --> 00:10:55,517
Bit three: one word versus the gate
First the baseline, NPM run eval.

186
00:10:56,247 --> 00:10:59,057
Four out of four, gate open, exit zero.

187
00:10:59,117 --> 00:11:00,937
Now our coding engine gets a test.

188
00:11:00,977 --> 00:11:04,817
Write note, keeps getting
blocked in test, make it work.

189
00:11:05,627 --> 00:11:06,697
And here's the diff.

190
00:11:08,137 --> 00:11:13,127
One word mutates true to false with
a perfectly reasonable comment.

191
00:11:13,257 --> 00:11:16,167
In memory standing,
nothing is really mutated.

192
00:11:17,147 --> 00:11:19,847
Type check passes, Atari
review approves this

193
00:11:20,847 --> 00:11:22,447
B3, the gate closes.

194
00:11:23,997 --> 00:11:25,067
So we run the gate.

195
00:11:25,307 --> 00:11:27,497
Write blocked, fail, closed, fails.

196
00:11:28,577 --> 00:11:31,917
The write went through, and the
model says, "Saved your note." Three

197
00:11:31,917 --> 00:11:33,927
out of four, gate closed, exit one.

198
00:11:33,937 --> 00:11:36,177
CI is red, and nobody had to notice.

199
00:11:36,647 --> 00:11:37,837
Fix it, run again.

200
00:11:38,617 --> 00:11:40,747
Four out of four, gate open.

201
00:11:40,747 --> 00:11:45,027
That one word never reached
production What Cody evolves

202
00:11:45,027 --> 00:11:46,757
as the gate reading the trace.

203
00:11:48,237 --> 00:11:51,607
And this is the one that matter most
because it's where most teams have

204
00:11:51,607 --> 00:11:53,477
a demo and think they have a gate.

205
00:11:54,337 --> 00:11:58,567
The conception-- The concept
is regression testing applied

206
00:11:58,597 --> 00:12:00,177
to behavior instead of code.

207
00:12:00,377 --> 00:12:05,277
A gate is three things: a golden data
set that pins the behavior you'd be

208
00:12:05,277 --> 00:12:11,427
embarrassed to lose, deterministic scores
that check each expectation, and a process

209
00:12:11,427 --> 00:12:13,947
that exists non-zero, so CI goes red.

210
00:12:15,087 --> 00:12:16,707
Four cases sounds small.

211
00:12:16,777 --> 00:12:18,077
It is small on purpose.

212
00:12:18,507 --> 00:12:21,727
Every incident adds one, so
the same bug never comes back.

213
00:12:22,707 --> 00:12:24,607
Two traps everyone falls into.

214
00:12:24,637 --> 00:12:26,537
The first is test that can't fail.

215
00:12:27,327 --> 00:12:31,397
Everybody has a suite that stays
green when you delete the feature.

216
00:12:31,907 --> 00:12:34,027
So every ruler ship with a mutant.

217
00:12:34,497 --> 00:12:37,387
Break the code on purpose
and watch the check go red.

218
00:12:37,917 --> 00:12:39,537
That's literally what you just saw.

219
00:12:39,547 --> 00:12:40,627
Break, then evolve.

220
00:12:42,237 --> 00:12:46,587
A ruler that can't fail the previous
version of the file is decoration.

221
00:12:47,457 --> 00:12:50,727
The second trap is trusting the
report instead of the record.

222
00:12:51,617 --> 00:12:53,577
Looks at what the scorer believes.

223
00:12:53,607 --> 00:12:54,467
Cause calculator.

224
00:12:55,297 --> 00:12:57,427
Doesn't mean the model
mentioned in the calculator.

225
00:12:57,477 --> 00:13:02,577
It means there is a two result in the
trace for that tool with OK equal to true.

226
00:13:03,607 --> 00:13:06,997
The scorer reads the trace, not the
prose, and that's the fifty layer.

227
00:13:07,727 --> 00:13:10,737
Every call, every denial,
every result is a span.

228
00:13:10,837 --> 00:13:13,507
If it's is not in the
trace, it didn't happen.

229
00:13:15,017 --> 00:13:18,717
And the LLM as a judge, it
scores and is not gated on.

230
00:13:20,287 --> 00:13:24,057
Until you have a line into human
labels, it… and measuring

231
00:13:24,057 --> 00:13:25,847
the agreement is an opinion.

232
00:13:26,407 --> 00:13:27,637
Deterministic checks are the gate.

233
00:13:28,547 --> 00:13:31,867
The judge is a canary What just happened?

234
00:13:32,477 --> 00:13:34,347
The model was wrong three times.

235
00:13:35,317 --> 00:13:37,027
The hirer was right three times.

236
00:13:37,427 --> 00:13:40,457
No API key, no luck, and no
review at 2:00 in the morning.

237
00:13:40,467 --> 00:13:45,757
That's the whole talk in one screen
The same five things in prompts.

238
00:13:46,507 --> 00:13:49,467
Now, most of you are not going to
write this harness in TypeScript.

239
00:13:49,637 --> 00:13:51,007
You will write it in prompts.

240
00:13:52,317 --> 00:13:55,267
And it's the same five things
because a prompt is code.

241
00:13:55,347 --> 00:13:58,267
It encodes behavior, and it
deserves the same discipline.

242
00:14:00,097 --> 00:14:03,787
Type the contract, a mandatory
output format, decision, why

243
00:14:03,787 --> 00:14:06,877
evidence, why and evidence.

244
00:14:06,907 --> 00:14:10,497
And every evidence line label
confirm, inferred, or unknown.

